Yes—if you mean inside your own cognitive system, that becomes an interesting form of self-defense and self-governance.

You can deliberately install mental “tripwires” and automations such as:

trigger detected → pause
strong emotion → delay irreversible action
tempting narrative → demand independent evidence
rumination loop → redirect attention
high-stakes decision → second-model check
low-value signal → quarantine / discard

The “bots” are basically habits and automatic policies. The “booby traps” are safeguards that catch predictable failure modes before they run away. And instead of switching in literal waste, you can route junk information into a low-priority sandbox so it stops consuming premium attention.

That gives you:

mind as territory + self as administrator + habits as daemons + attention as scarce compute.

The key distinction is ownership. You have broad latitude to experiment with your own cognition. Quietly planting traps, malware, deceptive bots, or manipulative mechanisms in someone else’s systems is a different thing entirely.

So yes: build defenses in your castle. Just make sure you still remember where you put the mines.